<td align="left" width="20px"><b>Answer:</b></td>
<td align="left"><b>dave brown</b> - 28/08/2005 06:34:33
</td></tr><tr><td>&nbsp;</td><td>I have heard of none, but...<br>
In doodling little gyro.s and force lines, my first fear was that if we were able to create an anti-grav., then we must be stealing so much gravity which is coming from the earth that it would leave the atmosphere directly above it unattracted. I then remembered that gravity attracts in all directions, so that the more gravity we steal into the gyro, the more it would hold the atmosphere in place.<br>
that said,<br>
I went on to think of escape velocity gyro.s, but they again would still fall to earth as they only wish to retain their position, in what ever space-time they are attracted to.<br>
that said,<br>
if matter has gravity, then anti-matter must have anti-gravity.<br>
<br>
Still working on stuff here. :)<br>
<br><br></td></tr>
<tr>
